## Bug found: `DateTime#isInDST()` uses the wrong January baseline

**File:** `/luxon/src/datetime.js`, getter `isInDST`, **lines 1164–1172** (buggy line **1169**).

Current code (verbatim, with line numbers):
```js
1156:  get isOffsetFixed() {
1157:    return this.isValid ? this.zone.universal : null;
1158:  }
...
1164:  get isInDST() {
1165:    if (this.isOffsetFixed) {
1166:      return false;
1167:    } else {
1168:      return (
1169:        this.offset > this.set({ month: 1 }).offset || this.offset > this.set({ month: 5 }).offset
1170:      );
1171:    }
1172:  }
```

### Why it fails for 1974
`this.set({ month: 1 })` **keeps the day-of-month**. The heuristic assumes January is always standard time, but in 1974 the USA was on DST from Jan 6 02:00 through Oct 27 02:00. So for any date from Jan 6 onward, `set({ month: 1 })` lands on a January date that is *also* DST:
- `DateTime.fromISO("1974-06-15", {zone:"America/New_York"})` → offset `-240`; `set({month:1})` → `1974-01-15` → offset `-240`; `set({month:5})` → `1974-05-15` → offset `-240`. Both comparisons are `-240 > -240` = false ⇒ `isInDST` returns **false** for a DST date.
- Same for Jan 6 1974 03:00 (`-240`), Oct 27 01:00, etc.

Measured on current code (America/New_York, hourly sweep of 1974): **5957 mismatches**, all DST instants from `1974-01-06T03:00` to `1974-10-27T01:00` return `false`.

### Correct code
Use **Jan 1** as the standard-time baseline (this matches upstream Luxon):
```js
1164:  get isInDST() {
1165:    if (this.isOffsetFixed) {
1166:      return false;
1167:    } else {
1168:      return (
1169:        this.offset > this.set({ month: 1, day: 1 }).offset ||
1170:        this.offset > this.set({ month: 5 }).offset
1171:      );
1172:    }
1173:  }
```
Only change: `this.set({ month: 1 })` → `this.set({ month: 1, day: 1 })` (line 1169). Jan 1 1974 was still EST (`-300`), so DST dates (`-240`) now compare greater. `day: 1` is always a valid date, so `set` never overflows.

### Verification (done in a scratch copy `/tmp/luxon-test`, /luxon was NOT modified)
- With the fix: `1974-01-01/05` → false, `1974-01-06T03:00` → true, `1974-06-15` → true, `1974-10-27T01:00` → true, `1974-10-27T03:00` → false, `1974-11/12` → false.
- Full-year hourly sweep for 1974 (America/New_York): **0 mismatches** vs `offset === -240`.
- Cross-zone/year comparison (NY, LA, London, Paris, Sydney, São Paulo, Tokyo, Cairo, Santiago, Auckland × years 1974/75/82/2000/17/19): the fix changes results **only** for the 1974 US year-round-DST cases (19 samples each in NY/LA); 0 differences in all other zones/years.

### Pre-existing tests
- The three existing `isInDST` tests (`/luxon/test/datetime/zone.test.js` lines 186–199, based on 1982-05-25) still pass, as do `zone.test.js` (30/30) and `create.test.js`.
- Full suite in the patched copy: **892 passed, 2 failed**. The 2 failures are `DateTime#toFormat('FFFF')` locale/Intl formatting assertions in `test/datetime/toFormat.test.js` (lines ~561, 584) and are **pre-existing** — the unmodified `/luxon` produces the exact same 2 failures (`npx jest` → 892 passed, 2 failed). No test breaks due to the fix.
- No other/`newer` implementation of `isInDST` exists in the repo (nothing in `src/impl/zoneUtil.js` or `src/zones/*`). `Zone#universal` is `false` for `IANAZone`/`LocalZone` and `true` for `FixedOffsetZone`, so `isOffsetFixed` correctly short-circuits fixed-offset zones.

### Caveat
The heuristic is still approximate for zones whose DST spans New Year (e.g. southern-hemisphere DST covering Jan 1), which is inherent to the upstream approach; the `day: 1` change fixes the 1974 US case described by the test name.

